{"meta": {"chapter_title": "Foundations of CBI and Design Tensions", "title": "DTS Map Reading", "description": "CAT 531 textbook section connecting dts map reading to Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.", "course": "cat531-supplement", "chapter": "01", "section": "03", "learning_objectives": ["Analyze how the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.", "Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.", "Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.", "Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible."], "concept_ids": ["cat531_supplement_foundations_of_cbi_and_design_tensions", "cat531_supplement_dts_map_reading", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"], "prereq_section_ids": ["cat531-supplement/01/02"], "practice_ids": ["cat531_supplement_1_3_dts_map_reading_artifact_signal", "cat531_supplement_1_3_dts_map_reading_annotation_signal", "cat531_supplement_1_3_dts_map_reading_ai_boundary", "cat531_supplement_1_3_dts_map_reading_calibration"], "estimated_time_minutes": 14, "order": 3, "top_tier_hardened": false}, "content": "# DTS Map Reading\n\n![DTS Map Reading textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat531-supplement/01-03-dts-map-reading.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nA Design Tension Studio map looks finished the moment it has boxes and arrows. That is exactly the trap. A map can be neat, color-coded, and completely unreadable as an argument, because the reader cannot tell from the picture how the values conflict or what was actually given up. Reading a map well is a separate skill from drawing one, and it is the skill a reviewer, a mentor, or a grade team will use to judge your reasoning.\n\nThis section matters because the same diagram supports very different decisions depending on how its parts are labeled. Two value nodes joined by an edge labeled \"tension\" assert that a conflict exists but explain nothing; a decision node that says \"Use Tool X\" hides whether anything was traded. Learning to audit nodes and edges turns a map from a decoration into evidence that another professional can inspect, question, and improve.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA colleague hands you a tidy DTS map about adopting an automated essay scorer: two value nodes, one edge between them labeled \"tension,\" and a decision node that reads \"Use AutoScore.\" It looks complete. But you cannot answer the only questions that matter: how do the two values conflict, and what did the decision give up? The map asserts a conflict and a choice while hiding the mechanism of both.\n\nProfessional judgment becomes visible only when each part of the map is readable on its own. The opening task of this section is to learn what to interrogate, node by node, edge by edge.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nReading a DTS map is an audit of three component types, each with a failure mode you learn to catch:\n\n- **Value nodes** name what is at stake. Failure mode: a node that is actually a tool or a feeling (\"AutoScore,\" \"frustration\") rather than a learning value. Fix: restate it as something you want for students (\"fair feedback for multilingual writers\").\n- **Edges** name how two values conflict. Failure mode: a generic label like \"tension\" or \"vs.\" that hides the mechanism. A readable edge names the causal link: \"AutoScore is fast but was trained on native-speaker prose, so it penalizes multilingual sentence patterns.\" The edge is where most maps fail, because drawing a line is easy and explaining the conflict is hard.\n- **Decision nodes** name the negotiated choice. Failure mode: a node that states a choice (\"Use AutoScore\") but hides the trade. A readable decision node states what was adopted, what was yielded, and why: \"Use AutoScore for first-draft turnaround only; teacher re-scores flagged multilingual essays. Yielded: full automation.\"\n\nThe reading test is simple: every edge must name a mechanism, and every decision node must make a trade and its evidence visible. [TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge) helps you check that the value nodes are real learning values rather than tool names; critical edtech studies helps you check that the edge names a real cost rather than a vague worry. A map passes when a stranger can reconstruct the conflict and the trade without you explaining it.\n\n<inline-check question=\"An edge between two value nodes is labeled only the word tension. What is the failure mode?\" explanation=\"A generic label like tension or vs. asserts that a conflict exists but hides the mechanism. The reader cannot tell HOW the two values conflict. A readable edge names the causal link, for example that AutoScore is fast but was trained on native-speaker prose, so it penalizes multilingual phrasing.\" options='[{\"text\":\"The edge hides the causal mechanism of the conflict\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"The edge has too many words\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The edge should point to a tool, not a value\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Nothing is wrong; the bare word tension is a complete edge label\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\nMatch each map component to the specific failure mode you must catch when auditing it.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"match\" items='[{\"term\":\"Value node\", \"definition\":\"Is actually a tool or a feeling (AutoScore, frustration) rather than a learning value\"}, {\"term\":\"Edge\", \"definition\":\"Carries a generic label like tension or vs. that hides the causal mechanism of the conflict\"}, {\"term\":\"Decision node\", \"definition\":\"States a chosen winner but hides what was yielded and the evidence behind it\"}]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nScenario: you are reading the colleague's DTS map from the opening case. Reading a map well means auditing its nodes and edges, not admiring its layout. Walk it in four numbered moves.\n\n**Before (the peer's map as drawn).**\n\n> - Value node: \"Faster feedback\"\n> - Value node: \"Fair feedback for multilingual writers\"\n> - Edge between them, labeled: \"tension\"\n> - Decision node: \"Use AutoScore\" (no trade-off note)\n\n1. **Audit the value nodes.** Both are legitimate learning values. Keep both.\n2. **Audit the edge.** The label is just \"tension.\" That is unreadable. The reader cannot tell HOW the values conflict. Annotate it: \"AutoScore returns scores in seconds (faster feedback) but trained on native-speaker prose, so it penalizes multilingual sentence patterns (fairness cost).\"\n3. **Audit the decision node.** \"Use AutoScore\" states a choice but hides what was yielded. A readable decision node reads: \"Use AutoScore for first-draft turnaround only; teacher re-scores flagged multilingual essays by hand.\"\n4. **Check evidence.** The decision node should cite what informed it: a 12-essay spot check showing AutoScore marked 4 of 5 multilingual essays a full band lower than the teacher did.\n\n**After (the map made readable).**\n\n> - Edge label: \"AutoScore is fast but penalizes multilingual phrasing (12-essay check: 4/5 scored a band low).\"\n> - Decision node: \"Adopt AutoScore for first-draft speed; teacher hand-re-scores any essay it flags below proficient. Yielded: full automation. Reason: speed gain does not justify systematic under-scoring of multilingual writers.\"\n\nThe map is now readable because every edge names a mechanism and the decision node makes the negotiation and its evidence inspectable, instead of asserting a choice with no visible trade.\n\nPredict each audit move before you reveal it. The map under audit has two value nodes (\"Faster feedback,\" \"Fair feedback for multilingual writers\"), an edge labeled \"tension,\" and a decision node reading \"Use AutoScore.\"\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Auditing the AutoScore map, node by node\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"You audit the two value nodes first. What should you do with them?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Restate both as tools\",\"Keep both; they are legitimate learning values\",\"Delete the fairness node to simplify\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Both faster feedback and fair feedback for multilingual writers are genuine learning values. Keep both; the conflict between them is real.\"},{\"prompt\":\"You reach the edge labeled with the bare word tension. What does a readable edge require?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"A shorter label\",\"A named causal mechanism of the conflict\",\"A color change\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Annotate the mechanism: AutoScore returns scores in seconds (faster feedback) but was trained on native-speaker prose, so it penalizes multilingual sentence patterns (fairness cost).\"},{\"prompt\":\"The decision node says only Use AutoScore. What is missing?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"What was yielded and why\",\"A second tool option\",\"The vendor name\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"A readable decision node states what was adopted, what was yielded, and the reason: use AutoScore for first-draft turnaround only; teacher re-scores flagged multilingual essays; yielded full automation.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Finally, cite the evidence that informed the call: a 12-essay spot check showing AutoScore marked 4 of 5 multilingual essays a full band lower than the teacher. Now a stranger can reconstruct both the conflict and the trade from the map alone.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nWhen auditing a map, read the value conflict first, the tool's affordance second, and the policy boundary third. A tool choice is justified only when it protects students while improving a specific learning activity, and the map must show that protection on its face.\n\nUse this check when a map looks finished but the reasoning may still be hidden:\n\n1. **Purpose check:** Are the value nodes real learning values, or are they tool names and feelings in disguise?\n2. **Evidence check:** Does each edge name a mechanism, and does the decision node cite the observation that informed it?\n3. **Revision check:** Does the decision node state what was yielded and why, or does it merely announce a choice?\n\nIf any edge says only \"tension,\" the map is not yet readable.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe frequent error is to write a general reflection about the map (\"this was a hard trade-off\") instead of repairing the unreadable parts of the map itself. Reflection is not a substitute for a legible edge. A second error is admiring layout: a beautifully arranged map with vague labels reads worse than an ugly one with precise labels. A third is accepting a decision node that names a winner without naming a loser; a trade with nothing yielded is not a negotiation, and a reader should distrust it.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply map-reading to a new artifact: a peer's DTS map about adopting an AI attendance-and-engagement camera that flags \"disengaged\" students. Read it as an auditor.\n\nAudit the value nodes. One reads \"engagement,\" the other reads \"the camera.\" The second is a tool, not a value; restate it as the value it threatens: \"student privacy and trust.\" Now both nodes are learning values that can genuinely conflict.\n\nAudit the edge, which is labeled \"balance.\" That hides the mechanism entirely. A readable edge names the causal cost: \"the camera infers engagement from face and posture, which misreads neurodivergent and culturally varied expression, so it surveils students to chase an unreliable signal.\" Critical edtech studies supplies exactly this mechanism.\n\nAudit the decision node, which says \"Use camera in pilot.\" It announces a choice and yields nothing. Rewrite it so a reader sees the trade and its evidence: \"Decline the camera; pilot a two-question exit ticket instead. Yielded: automated, continuous data. Reason: a 3-day trial flagged 5 attentive neurodivergent students as disengaged, so the signal is not trustworthy enough to justify constant surveillance.\" The walkthrough shows the same audit, applied to a different conflict, turning an opaque map into one a reviewer can act on.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, keep a two-column note: each **map component** on the left, the **question it must answer to be readable** on the right. For a value node: is this a learning value or a tool name? For an edge: does it name a mechanism? For a decision node: does it name what was yielded? Mark every component that fails its question; those marks are your revision list.\n\nA strong reader of DTS maps can answer four questions:\n\n1. Are all value nodes genuine learning values rather than tools or feelings?\n2. Does every edge explain the causal mechanism of the conflict?\n3. Does the decision node state the trade and cite the evidence behind it?\n4. Could a stranger reconstruct the negotiation from the map alone?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe map is strong when a mentor can read it cold: value nodes are learning values, every edge names a mechanism, and the decision node states what was adopted, what was yielded, and the evidence behind it. The most common missing layer is the edge mechanism; if yours says only \"tension,\" mark it and ask BigAL to compare a generic edge label against a mechanism-naming one rather than for a general explanation.\n\nA useful decision-node line sounds like: \"Adopt for first-draft speed only; teacher hand-re-scores flagged essays. Yielded full automation, because the speed gain does not justify under-scoring multilingual writers (12-essay check: 4 of 5 a band low).\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the essay-scorer map into a class of confident, monolingual writers preparing for a timed standardized exam. The value nodes stay (fast feedback, fair feedback), but the edge mechanism weakens: if the scorer's training register matches the students, the fairness cost shrinks, and the decision node might legitimately read \"adopt fully.\" If you cannot rewrite the edge and decision node for this new population, you were reading the map's picture rather than its argument.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat531-supplement/01-03-dts-map-reading-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision\" course=\"CAT 531\" section=\"01.03\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision for CAT 531 01.03. A peer comment points to a evidence move issue connected to critical edtech studies. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which trace would make the revision easiest to review?\" options='[\"A before-after artifact record linked to the decision you made and the evidence you used.\", \"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"0\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_dts_map_reading\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision captures instructional purpose, equity or privacy boundary, support request, revision rationale, and confidence change.\n\n## Concept Map\n\nSee how auditing nodes and edges connects to evidence-based revision and the reading anchors.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat531_supplement_foundations_of_cbi_and_design_tensions\", \"cat531_supplement_dts_map_reading\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## References\n\n- [TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge). https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x\n- critical edtech studies. https://doi.org/10.4324/9781315670160\n- [AI and education datafication](https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017)\n- teacher professional judgment. https://doi.org/10.1177/002205741319300304\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "cat531_supplement_1_3_dts_map_reading_artifact_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat531_supplement_dts_map_reading", "stem": "A DTS map shows two value nodes ('Personalized pacing' and 'Shared class discussion') connected to a single decision node ('Adopt self-paced AI tutor'). The decision node lists no trade-off note. What is a reader's first concern?", "options": ["The map has too many nodes.", "The map asserts a decision but does not show how the two competing values were negotiated, so the reader cannot tell what was traded away.", "Personalized pacing is not a real value.", "Maps should never connect two values to one decision."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Reading a DTS map means checking that the decision node makes the negotiation visible. A decision with no trade-off note hides which value was favored and at what cost.", "misconception_id": "cat531_supplement_1_3_map_decision_without_tradeoff", "difficulty": "easy", "concept_ids": ["cat531_supplement_foundations_of_cbi_and_design_tensions", "cat531_supplement_dts_map_reading", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"]}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_1_3_dts_map_reading_annotation_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat531_supplement_dts_map_reading", "stem": "On a peer's DTS map, an edge is labeled only with the word 'tension' between 'Equity' and 'Efficiency.' What annotation would most improve the map's readability?", "options": ["Replace the edge label with a specific claim about HOW efficiency pressures equity here, for example 'auto-grading speeds feedback but flags multilingual phrasing as errors.'", "Delete the edge so the two values are not connected.", "Add five more value nodes to balance the map.", "Change the word 'tension' to 'conflict' with no other change."], "correct_index": 0, "explanation": "A readable DTS edge names the mechanism of the tension, not just that one exists. Specifying how efficiency pressures equity lets a reader evaluate the actual conflict.", "misconception_id": "cat531_supplement_1_3_edge_label_names_no_mechanism", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["cat531_supplement_dts_map_reading", "evidence_based_revision"]}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_1_3_dts_map_reading_ai_boundary", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat531_supplement_ai_supported_learning", "stem": "A learner asks an AI to 'read this DTS map and tell me if my decision is correct.' The AI returns 'Your decision is correct.' Why is this an unreliable way to read a map?", "options": ["AI cannot process images of maps at all.", "DTS maps externalize a teacher's negotiated reasoning; correctness is not a property an AI can certify, so the AI output should be used to probe the map's gaps, not to grade it.", "The AI should have said 'incorrect' to be useful.", "Maps should only be read by the person who drew them."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "A DTS map is a reasoning trace, not a problem with a key. AI is defensibly used to surface gaps (an unlabeled edge, a missing trade-off) rather than to declare a negotiated judgment correct.", "misconception_id": "cat531_supplement_1_3_ai_certifies_map_correctness", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["ai_supported_learning", "cat531_supplement_dts_map_reading"]}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_1_3_dts_map_reading_calibration", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat531_supplement_dts_map_reading", "stem": "Two DTS maps describe the same classroom dilemma. Map A is dense, colorful, and has 14 nodes; Map B has 4 nodes but each decision node states the value favored, the value yielded, and the evidence. A reviewer should judge Map B stronger because:", "options": ["Map B uses fewer colors.", "Readability of a DTS map comes from making the negotiation and evidence inspectable, not from node count or visual density.", "Map A has too many nodes to be legal.", "Map B is shorter to read."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "A well-read or well-built DTS map is judged by whether a reviewer can reconstruct the negotiated judgment and its evidence, not by visual richness. Density can hide that the trade-offs were never made explicit.", "misconception_id": "cat531_supplement_1_3_density_read_as_quality", "difficulty": "hard", "concept_ids": ["cat531_supplement_dts_map_reading", "evidence_based_revision"]}]}, "content_version": {"content_version": "8f88caa79f05d366622d64ac86cf9f2328e212338e109c5dcee041a52abe71cf", "algorithm": "sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1", "parts": ["mdx", "meta", "practice", "misconceptions"]}, "misconceptions": [{"id": "cat531_supplement_1_3_map_decision_without_tradeoff", "pattern": "decision_node_hides_tradeoff", "description": "Reading a DTS decision node as complete even when it states no trade-off between the competing values", "trigger": "learner accepts a decision node that names a choice but not what value was yielded", "feedback": "Check the decision node for the trade-off: which value was favored, which was yielded, and at what cost. A decision with no trade-off note is not yet readable.", "rail_action": "ask"}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_1_3_edge_label_names_no_mechanism", "pattern": "edge_label_without_mechanism", "description": "Labeling a tension edge with a bare word like 'tension' instead of the mechanism by which the two values conflict", "trigger": "learner reads or writes an edge labeled only 'tension' or 'conflict' with no explanation of how", "feedback": "Name the mechanism on the edge: how does one value pressure the other in this specific classroom situation?", "rail_action": "represent"}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_1_3_ai_certifies_map_correctness", "pattern": "ai_grades_negotiated_map", "description": "Treating an AI's verdict on a DTS map as a correctness certification rather than a prompt to find gaps", "trigger": "learner asks AI whether a map's decision is correct and treats the answer as authoritative", "feedback": "A map is a reasoning trace, not a keye